{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1394903-could-changeling-the-dreaming-be-taken-as-a-metaphor-for-neurodivergence","id":1394903,"title":"Could Changeling: the Dreaming be taken as a metaphor for neurodivergence?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-07T17:36:36-07:00","id":1394903,"title":"Could Changeling: the Dreaming be taken as a metaphor for neurodivergence?","content":"Changelings literally see the world in a different way, thanks to chimera reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T17:53:32-07:00","id":1394915,"title":"","content":"I can't speak for anyone's experience but my own or what the intentions of any of the developers are/were but just speaking for myself I think there are useful experiences that can be achieved with looking at CtD through that lense.<br /> <br /> So, I've struggled with depression throughout my adult life. I'm on medication now and I'm generally doing a lot better, but when I think about some of my experiences, I definitely feel that how changelings experience Banality as the slow destruction of your passion and identity under a crushing sense of numbness, I definitely think that there's a story to be told about Depression with Changeling the Dreaming and its Banality mechanic.<br /> <br /> Similarly, there's the central nature of the balancing act between Bedlam and Banality. You can't entirely deny the harshness of the world and you can't fully cloak yourself in the things you most enjoy and ignore everything else, you just won't survive. Neither can you survive by turning your back on your passions. That's a balancing act I think that anyone can relate to, even if it may be particularly poignant for certain people who are nuerodivergent.<br /> <br /> Anyway though, that's just my experience. Yeah though, I think that banality and how it can wear away at you until nothing left is apathy and gray could be used excellently as a metaphor for depression.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T20:02:21-07:00","id":1394947,"title":"","content":"I agree with Gryffon15 (as I'm on medication for depression), but I don't know if depression falls into the label &quot;neurodivergence&quot;...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;No, no, don't look any further, my profile is actually more handsome than me&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3586-alqamar-alaswad","name":"Alqamar Alaswad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3586_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"287"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T20:06:54-07:00","id":1394952,"title":"","content":"That's a fair point, I don't know if it does either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T20:18:19-07:00","id":1394961,"title":"","content":"Many of the World of Darkness game lines put a nice spin on actual issues. Werewolf, for example, takes the idea of teenage runaways and romanticizes it by asking, &quot;What if these people found a real family who appreciated them for what they are and welcomed them&quot; instead of the actual, sad reality of what happens to most runaways. Likewise, Changeling dealt with mental health issues - what if the schizophrenic wasn't mad, but saw the world as it truly is and that world gave them a home. As such, similar issues like neurodiversity also fit in.<br /> <br /> In the little work I've put into my chronicles for Changeling (which is only as crossover elements to other games, never a Changeling chronicle itself), I usually try to work in a facade of a Changeling's mortal seeming by including how others view that person - do they view that person as mentally ill, a &quot;free spirit&quot;, or some other acknowledgment that they're &quot;different&quot;.<br /> <br /> So I definitely think that can be used as one element in a Changeling chronicle. Not the only one. But it's a valid view or inspiration that can be incorporated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T20:21:43-07:00","id":1394962,"title":"","content":"Additionally, there is this actual in-game element: some Dauntain and Autumn People work as mental healthy professionals, specially therapists... which is quite scary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;No, no, don't look any further, my profile is actually more handsome than me&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3586-alqamar-alaswad","name":"Alqamar Alaswad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3586_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"287"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T23:07:26-07:00","id":1395014,"title":"","content":"A case could be made that you need to tread lightly where matters of mental illness are concerned. While I think that Onyx Path sometimes goes too far in its tendency to excise the term \u201cMad\u201d (e.g., the recent Night Horrors supplement for Mage: the Awakening renamed a group that has been called the Mad for ages), there <i>is</i> something to the argument that it's far too easy to end up with unfortunate implications any time you equate something in the game with a mental illness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-07T23:16:35-07:00","id":1395017,"title":"","content":"E.g. Some unfortunate snippets and implications of Clanbook: Malkavian 1st edition...<br /> <br /> And this one and similar pages in TV tropes:<br /> <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/PowerBornOfMadness\" target=\"_blank\">https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.p...rBornOfMadness</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;No, no, don't look any further, my profile is actually more handsome than me&quot;","edited":{"userid":3586,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3586-alqamar-alaswad","user":"Alqamar Alaswad","date":". 06-08-2020, 02:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3586-alqamar-alaswad","name":"Alqamar Alaswad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3586_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"287"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T15:52:42-07:00","id":1395165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1394903-could-changeling-the-dreaming-be-taken-as-a-metaphor-for-neurodivergence?p=1395014#post1395014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A case could be made that you need to tread lightly where matters of mental illness are concerned. While I think that Onyx Path sometimes goes too far in its tendency to excise the term \u201cMad\u201d (e.g., the recent Night Horrors supplement for Mage: the Awakening renamed a group that has been called the Mad for ages), there <i>is</i> something to the argument that it's far too easy to end up with unfortunate implications any time you equate something in the game with a mental illness.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <u><i>Neurodivergence</i></u> is a broad term that encompasses &quot;atypical&quot; varieties of neurological development, including but not limited to the autistic spectrum, dyslexia, dysgraphia, and any number of other forms. As I understand it, this is distinct from mental illness - neurodivergence is characterized by a brain that is &quot;wired differently&quot; at birth or in early development, where mental illnesses tend to develop later from some combination of neurochemical malfunction and situational factors. I'd say that the pitfalls of dealing with neurodivergence from a gaming perspective have more to do with identity - someone who has a mild autism or dyslexia might not like being treated as if they have a &quot;disorder,&quot; and if they don't need continual assistance, they might very well have a good point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;</span>Explanations exist; they have existed for all time; there is always a well-known solution to every human problem \u2014 neat, plausible, and wrong.<span style=\"font-size:14px\">&quot;<br /> - H.L. Mencken</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/467-bob-the-skull","name":"Bob the Skull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar467_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"71"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T16:02:18-07:00","id":1395166,"title":"","content":"Do learning disabilities count?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T16:56:20-07:00","id":1395179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1394903-could-changeling-the-dreaming-be-taken-as-a-metaphor-for-neurodivergence?p=1395166#post1395166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do learning disabilities count?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T18:43:23-07:00","id":1395195,"title":"","content":"Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T20:35:44-07:00","id":1395223,"title":"","content":"You're right. I'm not terribly familiar with the term \u201cneurodivergence\u201d; but I'm familiar with the concept. There's a book called \u201cthe Dyslexic Advantage\u201d that's all about the notion that dyslexia is only a learning disability when you approach it wrong \u2014 that it's like looking through a telescope from the wrong end and concluding that it's defective. It then goes into detail about some remarkable abilities that tend to manifest in people with dyslexia, and how those talents are actually beneficial manifestations of the exact same \u201cwiring\u201d that also manifests as dyslexia. <br /> <br /> A fairly minor example, all things considered, is that dyslexics frequently have a strong sense of spatial relationships; in fact, they're so adept at what a friend of mine calls \u201c3d Tetris\u201d that they frequently flip the shapes of letters around without realizing that they've done it \u2014 thus a tendency to get b and d mixed up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T04:28:04-07:00","id":1395295,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> Interesting. I have a visual learning disability and I\u2019m the exact opposite. I\u2019m good with words and social interaction but I suck at spatial relationships.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T06:25:47-07:00","id":1395329,"title":"","content":"The point is that you might <i>not</i> have a \u201clearning disability\u201d; what you may have is a brain that's wired to learn in a way that doesn't match up all that well with the standard teaching methods in use today. An alternate approach to teaching that's designed to align with the way you learn (whatever that is) might prove much more effective than trying to require you to learn things through the usual teaching model. <br /> <br /> I speak in generalities here because I don't know you; and even if I did, I'm not an expert on these matters and couldn't diagnose you if I wanted to. But it's the basic idea that I'm trying to get across here: rather than thinking of it as a learning disability, try to find out what your ideal mode of learning is and find ways to play to that strength.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T09:22:35-07:00","id":1395366,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> maybe that\u2019s why I learn better from talking to people than being lectured at? And why I remember historical stuff I read in novels and gaming books better than from textbooks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T10:09:43-07:00","id":1395387,"title":"","content":"I think you'd have to classify anyone that learns better via lectures as the neurodivergent. Lectures are, flat out, the least effective form of teaching, the vast, vast majority of human brains learn best by interacting with the subject matter, and lectures are the least interactive method of conveying things. Text books are just as bad (novels want you to interact with the text via imaging the events, gaming books are there to give you a framework for actively engaging in topics).<br /> <br /> That said... I'm kind of uncomfortable with the idea of CtD as metaphor for neurodivergence. Mostly because I don't see how you handle such a complicated topic in a single metaphor without invariably making the metaphor something dangerous.<br /> <br /> My therapist and my meds don't destroy my creativity, my brain self-sabotages my creative efforts and those supports let me express myself better. My life is not a balance between creative chaos and banal order. My life is a struggle to find the order in the chaos so that I can thrive.<br /> <br /> But so what works as a metaphor for my neurodivergence isn't going to work for other people.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}